/-! Foundation agreement -- the six declarations the model shares with the ambient environment.

The six declarations the model shares with any ambient Clean environment --
`Nat`, `Nat.add`, `Nat.pred`, `Nat.sub`, `Eq` and `Empty` -- restated as their own
DEFINING EQUATIONS, each held by `Eq.refl` so the kernel must decide it by
computation.

Why this file exists. `crates/clean-verify` builds the model on an EMPTY
environment and therefore declares its own `Nat`/`Eq`/`Empty`; a `clean { }`
island registers into the compiler PRELUDE's environment, which already has
them. In the spec these equations are trivially true -- they restate the model's
own bodies. In an island they are a real check on the ambient environment, and
they FAIL unless the prelude's versions compute exactly as the model's do. Same
text, two environments, two kernels: that is what makes an island green mean
something about the proved model rather than about whatever the prelude happens
to hold.

AMBIENT NAMES. Besides the six, the model USES names it never declares -- measured,
exactly `Bool`, `Bool.true` and `Bool.false`, reached through
`is_const_app : KExpr -> Bool`. An ambient name is supplied by whichever environment
the model is elaborated in, so it needs a witness for the same reason a prelude-owned
declaration does, and it gets one below. A NEW ambient dependency introduced by a
future unit would not be caught automatically; deriving the ambient set mechanically
is the next step, and is recorded as such rather than assumed away.

WHY THESE SIX. `scripts/model_islands.py --verify` probes every one of the
model's 24 declarations against the island environment. Exactly six answer
"Duplicate declaration", i.e. the prelude already owns them: `Nat`, `Nat.add`,
`Nat.pred`, `Nat.sub`, `Eq`, `Empty`. The other eighteen are free and are
registered by the units themselves. The six cannot be re-declared -- an
`inductive` re-declaration naming a subset of the prelude's constructors is
SILENTLY accepted and registers nothing, and a `def` re-declaration is refused
outright -- so agreement is the only honest thing to check about them.

THE TRANSCRIPTION IS CLOSED. These equations were read off the model units by
hand, so on their own they would prove only that the PRELUDE satisfies whatever
was written down. The other side is
`crates/clean-verify/src/spec/model_units/agreement.rs`, which registers THIS
FILE into the model's own foundation (`CoreSpecBundle::ModelUnits` -- the spec's
private `Nat`/`Eq`/`Empty` over an EMPTY environment) through the same
registration path the units use, and requires every equation to elaborate. An
equation that is not the model's fails there. Both sides also run the same five
false equations as negative controls and require each to be REFUSED, because
"every equation elaborates" is satisfiable by an equation that says nothing.

So: one text, two environments, two kernels. Trust's kernel decides it over the
prelude when the Rust crate is built; Clean's kernel decides it over the model in
`cargo test -p clean-verify --lib -- spec::model_units::agreement`. -/

/-- Agreement: the ambient Nat has the model's zero constructor. -/
def fa_nat_zero : Nat := Nat.zero

/-- Agreement: the ambient Nat has the model's succ constructor, at its type. -/
def fa_nat_succ : Nat -> Nat := Nat.succ

/-- Agreement: Nat.add recurses on its RIGHT argument, base case (model equation 1). -/
def fa_nat_add_zero (n : Nat) : @Eq Nat (Nat.add n Nat.zero) n := @Eq.refl Nat n

/-- Agreement: Nat.add recurses on its RIGHT argument, step case (model equation 2). -/
def fa_nat_add_succ (n : Nat) (m : Nat) : @Eq Nat (Nat.add n (Nat.succ m)) (Nat.succ (Nat.add n m)) := @Eq.refl Nat (Nat.succ (Nat.add n m))

/-- Agreement: Nat.pred sends zero to zero (model equation 1). -/
def fa_nat_pred_zero : @Eq Nat (Nat.pred Nat.zero) Nat.zero := @Eq.refl Nat Nat.zero

/-- Agreement: Nat.pred peels one successor (model equation 2). -/
def fa_nat_pred_succ (n : Nat) : @Eq Nat (Nat.pred (Nat.succ n)) n := @Eq.refl Nat n

/-- Agreement: Nat.sub recurses on its RIGHT argument, base case (model equation 1). -/
def fa_nat_sub_zero (a : Nat) : @Eq Nat (Nat.sub a Nat.zero) a := @Eq.refl Nat a

/-- Agreement: Nat.sub peels the right operand through pred (model equation 2). -/
def fa_nat_sub_succ (a : Nat) (b : Nat) : @Eq Nat (Nat.sub a (Nat.succ b)) (Nat.pred (Nat.sub a b)) := @Eq.refl Nat (Nat.pred (Nat.sub a b))

/-- Agreement: Nat has EXACTLY the model's two constructors. The recursor's minor
premises are one per constructor, so a Nat carrying a third would not accept this
eliminator at this arity. Existence of zero and succ is pinned above; this is what
pins that there are no others. -/
def fa_nat_rec (C : Nat -> Type) (z : C Nat.zero) (s : (n : Nat) -> C n -> C (Nat.succ n)) (n : Nat) : C n := @Nat.rec C z s n

/-- Agreement: Eq is inhabited at reflexivity, at the model's constructor type. -/
def fa_eq_refl (T : Type) (a : T) : @Eq T a a := @Eq.refl T a

/-- Agreement: Eq has EXACTLY the model's one constructor, by the same
minor-premise-arity argument as Nat above. -/
def fa_eq_rec (T : Type) (a : T) (C : (b : T) -> @Eq T a b -> Prop) (r : C a (@Eq.refl T a)) (b : T) (h : @Eq T a b) : C b h := @Eq.rec T a C r b h

/-- Agreement: the ambient Bool has the model's true constructor. `Bool` is AMBIENT --
the model USES it (is_const_app returns one) and never declares it, so it is supplied
by whatever environment the model is elaborated in, and it needs a witness for the same
reason the prelude-owned declarations do. -/
def fa_bool_true : Bool := Bool.true

/-- Agreement: the ambient Bool has the model's false constructor. -/
def fa_bool_false : Bool := Bool.false

/-- Agreement: Bool has EXACTLY those two constructors, in that order, by the
minor-premise-arity argument used for Nat and Eq above. -/
def fa_bool_rec (C : Bool -> Type) (f : C Bool.false) (t : C Bool.true) (b : Bool) : C b := @Bool.rec C f t b

/-- Agreement: the shared types live at the model's SORT. The equations above pin
constructors, eliminator arity and computation, but say nothing about the sort the
type itself inhabits -- measured, an ambient `Empty : Prop` or `Bool : Type 2`
satisfies every one of them. These four pin it. -/
def fa_nat_sort : Type := Nat

/-- Agreement: Bool is at the model's sort. -/
def fa_bool_sort : Type := Bool

/-- Agreement: Empty is at the model's sort. -/
def fa_empty_sort : Type := Empty

/-- Agreement: Eq lands in Prop, not in Type. -/
def fa_eq_is_prop (T : Type) (a : T) (b : T) : Prop := @Eq T a b

/-- Agreement: Empty eliminates into any type, i.e. it has no constructors. -/
def fa_empty_elim (C : Type) (e : Empty) : C := @Empty.rec (fun (_ : Empty) => C) e
